<p>Diffusion models trained in semantic latent spaces converge much faster than pixel-space
models, but they inherit a decoder that caps fine detail. <strong>Semantic Detour Flow</strong> closes most
of that gap without any decoder: it is a pixel-space flow that follows frozen DINOv2 features
at high noise and hands over to pixels at low noise. One network, one velocity loss, and
sampling that runs entirely in pixel space.</p>

<div class="detour-num">
  <div><div class="v">92.8 → 38.8</div><div class="k">FID-10k, 20 epochs, same network</div></div>
  <div><div class="v">1.43</div><div class="k">FID-50k, ImageNet 256</div></div>
  <div><div class="v">1.59</div><div class="k">FID-50k, ImageNet 512</div></div>
  <div><div class="v">0.90 / 87.8</div><div class="k">GenEval / DPG-Bench, 512</div></div>
</div>

<figure class="detour-fig">
  <img src="/assets/img/detour/teaser.jpg" data-zoomable="" data-zoom-src="/assets/img/detour/full/teaser.jpg" alt="Samples from Semantic Detour Flow" />
</figure>

<h2 id="why-pixel-diffusion-is-slow">Why pixel diffusion is slow</h2>

<p>The gap is in the denoising target at high noise. When almost everything is noise, the best a
model can do is predict the posterior mean of the target given the noisy state. For pixels,
that posterior mean is a blur — an average over everything the image could have been. For
self-supervised features it is a <em>semantic prototype</em>: a representation that still says what
the thing is.</p>

<p>So a pixel model spends its early training budget learning to produce blur, while a latent
model spends the same budget learning content. That is the asymmetry, and it is the one thing
worth fixing.</p>

<h2 id="the-detour">The detour</h2>

<p>Let \(c(x)\) be the frozen DINOv2-B features of \(x\) on the 16×16 patch grid, centred and scaled to
standard deviation 0.5 — the scale of the image itself, so neither term dominates. We call it
the <strong>code</strong>. Let \(\Phi\) be the pixel shuffle that maps each 768-dimensional token into the
3×16×16 pixels of its patch, so \(\Phi c(x)\) is image-shaped and aligned with \(x\).</p>

<p>The noisy state mixes the image and the code, then adds noise:</p>

\[z_t = t\,m_t + (1-t)\,\varepsilon, \qquad m_t = \alpha_t\,x + \beta_t\,\Phi c(x).\]

<p>The weights hand the signal over from the code at \(t=0\) to the image at \(t=1\). We use a
trigonometric hand-over with a mid-path norm bump:</p>

\[(\alpha_t, \beta_t) = g_t\,(\sin\theta_t,\, \cos\theta_t), \qquad
\theta_t = \tfrac{\pi t}{2}, \qquad
g_t = 1 + \kappa \sin 2\theta_t,\]

<p>with \(\kappa = 0.25\). Plain pixel flow is the special case \(\alpha \equiv 1\), \(\beta \equiv 0\).
Differentiating gives the target velocity, and a single network is trained against it with one
unified loss — predicting pixels and code together.</p>

<p>DINOv2-B’s 768 channels are exactly one 16×16×3 patch, so \(\Phi\) is an isometry onto the image.
That coincidence is convenient, not essential; other encoders and code widths work too.</p>

<h3 id="watch-the-hand-over">Watch the hand-over</h3>

<p>The video runs the equation above continuously in \(t\), on a real image with its real code
(shown by its first three principal components). On the left is the signal \(m_t\) the path points
at; on the right the state \(z_t\) the network actually sees. Near noise the signal is a semantic
prototype; near data it is the photograph.</p>

